#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162729} :activity/id #uuid "851263af-22ca-51b7-b65b-78ee6f9ebb50" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048962 :topic/id #uuid "cfc8bab9-3c84-588c-af07-f2c8e11b8d36" :topic/math-academy-id 3079} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162729 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163366 :tutorial/content "We can use fraction models to multiply any two fractions. \n\nLet's figure out how to use models to compute the following product:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{5} \\times \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nWe start with the model of $\\dfrac{3}{5}.$\n\n![](images/06/06869533fb9eae08e88187c0965c9683c3b00e8cf269b121f416ce7f473ad508.png)\n\nSince we need to multiply by $\\dfrac{\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}3}$, we split the whole into $\\color{blue}3$ equal parts *horizontally*. But this time, we keep the shaded pieces in the bottom $\\color{red}2$ rows.\n\n![](images/0c/0c449cdff6dabfd6599925b0d450ab2d579ce49d2729b9a98fe63bb8992a850d.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we see that the shaded part represents $\\dfrac{6}{15}$ of the whole.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{5} \\times \\dfrac{2}{3} = \\dfrac{6}{15}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "98bc8936-f81c-50ef-a2f7-b9cb50262591" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8978 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a78e7754-a22c-59f9-9aa8-121a958efa96" :step/math-academy-id 135386 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162730}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162730 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161913 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161716 :question/id #uuid "432754ac-d9f0-5843-a751-b13821e2e725" :question/math-academy-id "e-14520"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3443d2b1-14c5-50ba-90b3-73b8bef47016" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050954}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162459} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427482} {:db/id #edb/ref 1